
The Compose intrinsics android declares are ordinary components, so a program
can call one directly to reach a composable no stdlib wrapper covers. The
generic emitter reads the declaration: the #[intrinsic] id is the composable's
name, each declared prop is the Compose argument of that name in declaration
order, and children are a trailing lambda. A childless intrinsic takes no
lambda, and a #id carried onto one becomes a testTag.

`chain` appends Modifier calls the Style has no field for. The window's own
content is kept out from under the status bar and the display cutout, last in
the chain, so it insets the content rather than the background. Text declares
`modifier` and `style` separately, so the Style behind the Modifier and the one
behind the TextStyle are distinct props.

The stdlib wrappers reach the same emitter: hbox's android override is written
in terms of Row and scroll's adds a chain entry, and both inline to the
intrinsic, so neither name survives into codegen. stack layers its children, so
it is a Box and not a Column.
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import android "sngl:platform/android"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    android.Column(chain=["fillMaxSize()"]) {
        android.Row #bar(modifier={padding=4}) {
            android.Spacer(modifier={height=8})
            android.Text(text="hi", modifier={padding=1}, style={fontSize=20})
        }
        hbox(style={padding=2}) {
            text(value="wrapped")
        }
        scroll {
            text(value="scrolled")
        }
        stack(style={padding=3}) {
            text(value="layered")
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
